      "report_date": "2026-11-06",
      "title": "Analysis of XCPE Malware and Its Observed Activity in Japan",
      "summary": "XCPE is malware preinstalled on some Android set-top boxes and other consumer devices such as streaming devices, sound bars, and projectors, and it can be used for remote command execution, information collection, and as a residential proxy exit node. Its C2 communication uses MQTT, with more than 40 remote actions defined, and it sends device and user data, including Google account email, Wi-Fi SSID, and LAN IP addresses, without TLS encryption. NICTs analysis found XCPE signed by Shenzhen Skyworth Digital Technology-linked certificates in addition to Videostrong, and implementations evolved toward proxy modules loaded externally, which may help evade detection. Observations counted about 1,900 infected devices in Japan out of roughly 800,000 worldwide, with the SviCloud 9P being the most common model. Google's takedown disabled the C2 infrastructure but did not remove the malware from devices, so defenders should check for an XCPE app, stop using affected devices, and isolate them from home networks.",
      "completeness": "Low",
      "threats": [
