      "title": "Armorsteal Unpacked: Breaking Down a Python Malware Kit That Steals Data and Mines Crypto",
      "summary": "Seqrite Labs analysed Armorsteal, a Python malware kit that ships with a portable Python 3.11 runtime and steals data while mining cryptocurrency on infected Windows machines. The kit's scripts were protected with the trial version of PyArmor, but its decryption key sits in the bundled runtime, so the researchers could unpack all of them. The controller, SysMon.py, picks a working server from 11 failover domains and runs only code carrying a valid RSA-2048 signature from the attacker. Its stealer modules target browser credentials, Discord tokens, crypto wallets, seed phrases, password manager vaults, and keystrokes, and it swaps copied crypto addresses. Defenders should note that the kit adds Windows Defender exclusions and hides its miner from monitoring tools, and that file hashes alone are a weak detection method, so behaviour such as Python running from C:\\Windows under scheduled tasks is more reliable.",
