      "title": "3AM Ransomware Affiliate Breaches UK Schools in Suspected Hybrid AI Intrusion",
      "summary": "3AM (also tracked as ThreeAM) is a Rust-based ransomware-as-a-service operation active since February 2023 that reportedly began as a fallback payload for failed LockBit deployments. In its latest activity, an affiliate breached a UK school estate of 47 systems spanning several unconnected Active Directory domains through an unknown access vector. The operator stole credentials from Veeam backup servers and domain controllers, created backdoor domain administrator accounts, and built two-way trusts between domains. Finance data was exfiltrated over an internal C2 channel before the encryptor hit about 37 machines in under half an hour. Command content strongly indicates language-model authorship, suggesting a human operator directing an AI agentic loop, though the toolkit itself is not novel. Defenders should audit domain trusts and privileged group membership, rotate exposed credentials, and hunt for the agentic loop artifacts described in the report.",
